    "outcome_summary": "Using TIMSS 2019 Grade 8 Science (44 countries; analytic sample after cleaning: 309,511), the study compared integrated vs. separated science curricula using propensity score matching across five plausible values of science achievement. Propensity score matching achieved near-perfect propensity score prediction accuracy on the test set (0.9998). After matching, Mann–Whitney U tests with Bonferroni correction indicated the integrated curriculum group had significantly higher science achievement than the separated group for each plausible value, but effect sizes were small (Cohen’s d ≈ 0.20–0.24). For direct prediction of science achievement from curriculum type and PPCT process/person covariates, random forest outperformed linear regression, lasso regression, decision trees, and neural networks, achieving the highest testing R² (0.51) and lowest testing RMSE (74.92). Feature importance from random forest showed socioeconomic background as the most important predictor, while curriculum type had a small average coefficient (0.010), indicating curriculum type was a weak direct predictor.",
    "research_design_type": "predictive",
    "null_fields_interpretation": null
  }
